{
  "abstract": "We study quintuples $\\{a,b,c,d_+,d_-\\}$ in which $d_\\pm=a+b+c+2abc\\pm2rst$ are both regular fourth elements of a triple. By Vieta, $d_+d_-+1=a^2+b^2+c^2-2ab-2ac-2bc-3$. In Lasić coordinates these form a threefold $V$ fibred in genus-one curves. Dujella, Kazalicki and Petričević (DKP) found infinitely many sextuples on the surface where the fibres split. We treat all of $V$. A sixth element regularly extending a sub-quadruple gives a double cover $C$ of the fibre, whose genus we bound exactly over $\\mathbb Q$ by counting branch points. On generic fibres $g(C)\\ge9$ (or $\\ge19$ for the second extension type). On two explicit rational curves one branch drops to $g\\ge6$, and on DKP's surface two branches have genus one. Odd-root counts can only fall under good reduction. So a complete scan of the parameter plane over $\\mathbb F_p$ gives $g(C)\\ge4$ on every rational fibre with good reduction at $p=101$, $211$, $223$ or $307$. A census of all fibres with parameters of height $\\le60$ finds 378 sextuples up to sign; 314 are absent from the corpora of rStation papers 5 and 9. It also finds three almost septuples (20 of 21 conditions) not on Dujella's list. Two have new regular-relation types, with local dimensions 3 and 2; the third shares the second's type. Both come from one construction: a regular chain $c_{-1},\\dots,c_2$ over a fixed pair, closed by a common regular fifth element. This gives a surface of almost septuples with at least two rational points. Of 25 known almost septuples, 14 contain such a quintuple, and only DKP's lies on the DKP surface. The height-certified Mordell–Weil sieve of paper 5 finds no seventh element of naive height $\\le B$, with $B\\ge7681$ (about 3300 digits), for any new sextuple. We also correct a statement in our paper 6. No septuple is found.",
